<Docs
  title="Agent pool"
  description="The quota-aware pool is Shepherd's next step: choose the next healthy installed agent when the preferred one is exhausted."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Callout tone="note" title="Pool management and task-start selection are here">
    Settings → Agent pool can inspect existing slots, reorder them, disable or enable them, correct a
    slot's limit and reset time, clear its cooldown, and force one slot for the next turn. A turn now
    asks the pool for a slot before it starts: the chosen slot and any skipped higher-priority slots
    are recorded in the transcript, and a configured pool that cannot serve the task stops the turn
    with its reasons instead of starting anyway.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="status">Status</h2>
  <p>
    <Badge tone="accent" variant="subtle">next</Badge> A pool slot is an installed agent plus one
    provider/model plus the credential or account it uses. Shepherd keeps the user's priority order,
    the local usage ledger, reset windows, cooldown state and last-used time. Credentials are
    referenced, never copied into the pool record or logs. Existing slots can be managed in
    Settings → Agent pool; a turn selects a slot for its agent before it starts.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why">Why a pool</h2>
  <p>
    Running several agents is useful until one hits a rate limit and you become the scheduler. The
    pool is meant to keep the workspace moving: when the preferred slot is exhausted, cooling down
    or unhealthy, the next healthy slot can take the task. The task must say which slot ran and which
    higher-priority slots it skipped, so the choice is visible rather than mysterious.
  </p>

  <h2 id="selection">How selection works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>A new task or conversation tries the user's preferred healthy slot.</li>
    <li>If that slot is exhausted, cooling down or otherwise unavailable, the next slot that can accept work takes the turn.</li>
    <li>An explicit task override wins over the priority order.</li>
    <li>After the turn settles plus an idle timeout (15 minutes by default), an idle reservation is released without losing a resumable session.</li>
    <li>A slot is never released during a running turn or a pending approval.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Selection is atomic across concurrent tasks, so two prompts cannot claim the same slot at once.
    If an adapter cannot resume after a release, the task says so and selects another slot for the
    next turn instead of pretending to continue.
  </p>

  <h2 id="visibility">What a task will show</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The selected slot, its provider/model and the agent's visible tier.</li>
    <li>Higher-priority slots that were skipped, with a visible reason: exhausted, cooling down, disabled or unhealthy.</li>
    <li>Known limit and reset time, or <strong>unknown</strong> when the provider has not exposed them.</li>
    <li>The current task, last error and last-used time in the pool UI.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Unknown capacity is shown as unknown, never as free. Shepherd must not claim a provider quota it
    cannot observe.
  </p>

  <h2 id="controls">Your controls</h2>
  <p>
    Settings → Agent pool shows each slot's provider/model, priority, availability, known limit and
    reset, current task, last error and last-used time. You can move a slot up or down, disable or
    enable it, correct its limit, unit and reset time, and clear its cooldown. The pool UI does not
    display credentials or invent a quota. A saved limit is a local correction, not a provider
    guarantee. One available slot can be forced for the next turn: the marker is session state, shown
    at the top of the panel with a clear action, and it is never written to the pool database. You
    can also import the catalog's agent-pool advisory snapshot to fill in a limit the app does not
    know; local usage and provider responses win, and a stale or missing record stays unknown. Each
    provider's own rule decides when a limit resets: Codex lists a 5-hour and a weekly window that
    restart from the slot's last observed use, and Claude lists the same two windows but reports the
    reset time itself, so the pool shows that reset instead of deriving one. A provider without a
    published rule reports no window rather than a guessed one, and no provider gets a hardcoded
    message count. When the pool selects a slot, every declared window must have headroom, and each
    window can be corrected separately.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">What it will not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It will not create accounts, buy capacity or store provider keys.</li>
    <li>It will not treat an unobserved quota as free or unlimited.</li>
    <li>It will not make a catalog or a website authoritative over the local ledger.</li>
    <li>It will not hide a fallback: the task records the slot that ran and the slots that were skipped.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The implementation contract, including the deterministic clock and failure cases, is on the
    <a href="/docs/contributors/quota-ledger">quota ledger</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2 id="today">Today</h2>
  <p>
    Use the agent picker per task and keep a terminal open when you need to switch manually. If pool
    slots already exist, Settings → Agent pool lets you inspect their status, reorder them, disable
    or enable them, correct their limits and reset times, clear a cooldown, force a slot for the next
    turn, see when each provider window reopens, and import a catalog advisory snapshot to seed an
    unknown limit. A turn now asks the pool for a slot, consumes a forced slot once, and records the
    chosen slot and skipped reasons in the transcript; when configured slots cannot take the turn it
    stops with those reasons. If an agent is missing or
    its adapter falls back, <a href="/docs/users/troubleshooting#agents">Troubleshooting</a> explains
    what to check. The <a href="/docs/users/releases">releases page</a> tracks the other next-step
    items.
  </p>
</Docs>
